\section{Summary}\label{sec:notation-summary}

This notation reference covers the symbols and conventions used throughout the book. The key conventions are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Contexts} ($\Gamma, \Delta$) are ordered lists of variable--type bindings.
  \item \textbf{Judgments} are written with $\vdash$: $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$ means ``in context $\Gamma$, expression $e$ has type $\tau$.''
  \item \textbf{Semantic brackets} $\SemBrack{-}$ denote the mathematical meaning of a syntactic construct.
  \item \textbf{Arrows} distinguish kinds of mapping: $\to$ for functions, $\longrightarrow$ for reduction, $\Rightarrow$ for derivation, $\implies$ for implication.
  \item \textbf{Superscripts} indicate variants: $\sharp$ for abstract, $op$ for opposite, $n$ for $n$-fold application.
\end{itemize}

When in doubt, consult the index for the first use of each symbol.

