% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Reading a Composition Formula as a Typed Path}

The notation \(g\circ f\) is read from right to left in the
categorical convention used here. Texts using diagrammatic order
may instead write the same path in traversal order. To quote a
formula from either convention, write down each arrow's type
first, then reconstruct the path explicitly. The rule catches
some, though not all, accidental reversals.

\textbf{Contract.} Given a nonempty chain of named arrows,
their declared source and target objects, and an explicit order
convention, return the type of the composite or the first
mismatched boundary in traversal order. Assume composition is
associative, object names refer to canonical objects, and no
implicit coercion or isomorphism conversion is permitted. This
check concerns composability, not equality between different
composites of the same type.

\begin{sudocode}[Normalize a printed chain to traversal order]
\Let{T}{\text{arrows in printed order}}
\If{convention is right-to-left}
  \To{Reverse arrows, never the direction of an arrow.}
  \Let{T}{\Call{Reverse}{T}}
\EndIf
\Let{s}{T[0].\mathrm{source}}
\Let{o}{s}
\For{each arrow $a$ in $T$}
  \To{The current object must match the next domain exactly.}
  \If{$o\ne a.\mathrm{source}$}
    \State \Return mismatch $(o,a,a.\mathrm{source})$
  \EndIf
  \Let{o}{a.\mathrm{target}}
\EndFor
\State \Return $s\to o$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Let \(f:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}\)
send \(n\) to \((n,n+1)\), and let \(g\) take the second
component into \(\mathbb{Z}\). The categorical \(g\circ f\)
traverses \(f,g\), has type \(\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}\),
and yields \(4\) on \(3\). Reading the printed list as
diagrammatic order also type-checks here, but yields the
different type
\(\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}\);
on \((3,9)\) it returns \((9,10)\).

After checking a prefix, \(o\) is its codomain and \(s\) its
original domain. The traversal is linear in the arrow count.
When every arrow is an endomorphism on the same object, both
conventions even give the same type; use a distinguishing
value test or a diagram to confirm which order was meant.

